## Conclusion

Claude Fable 5.1 is Anthropic's general-availability Mythos-tier model from their Claude family, released September 1, 2026, designed for complex, long-running agentic work, coding, and knowledge work. It features a massive 1M token context window, competitive pricing ($10/$50 per 1M tokens), and exceptional performance on agentic benchmarks (52.6% on Terminal-Bench-Science 0.1, 73.4% on CursorBench 3.2, 77.8% on Toolathon-Verified, 89.1% on SWE Multilingual, 67.4% on DeepSWE). The model ranks #1 on the RSI Index (35.03%), the highest score among all models, and costs 21% less than Claude Opus 5 ($1,481 vs $1,886). Fable 5.1 shares the same underlying architecture with Claude Mythos 5.1 but includes additional safety guardrails for general availability.

However, detailed public information about Claude Fable 5.1 is limited compared to other frontier models from Western companies. The model's strengths in agentic capabilities, RSI Index performance, and cost efficiency make it a strong contender in the current market, particularly for organizations needing sophisticated AI for complex, multi-hour workflows. The availability of Claude Opus 5.5 (released September 22, 2026) which offers 60% cheaper pricing and better performance on key agentic benchmarks suggests that Fable 5.1 may be a stepping stone rather than the final frontier model.

For organizations heavily invested in the Anthropic ecosystem and seeking Mythos-tier capabilities for complex, multi-hour workflows and research tasks, Claude Fable 5.1 could still be a viable option. For organizations requiring extensive documentation, benchmark validation, and third-party support, established models from OpenAI, Anthropic, or Google may offer better value and transparency. The higher pricing and availability of Claude Opus 5.5 also make it less attractive for organizations needing up-to-date information.

**Overall Assessment:** Exceptional agentic capabilities and RSI Index performance; however, limited public documentation and benchmark data compared to other frontier models, and newer models (Opus 5.5) offer better value and performance.

